(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-c-v1"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam (v1, archived) — Form C","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Succinylcholine with halothane causes rigidity, acidosis, tachycardia and fever. What is the treatment?","choices":["Pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase","Atropine to oppose muscarinic overactivity","Neostigmine to reverse the blockade","Rapid cooling and dantrolene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and blocking that release is what reduces heat production. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does guanethidine act?","choices":["It blocks transport of transmitters into storage vesicles","It blocks postsynaptic alpha-one receptors","It stimulates presynaptic alpha-two receptors","It blocks release of stored norepinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from reserpine's blockade of vesicular uptake. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which set gives the MAJOR indicators of atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Raised immunoglobulin E, positive allergy skin tests, and dry patchy scales","Nikolsky sign, mucosal erosion at two or more sites, and fever preceding the rash by one to three days","Comedones, papulopustular lesions, and nodules","Pruritus, rash in areas typical of the disease, chronic or repeated symptoms, and family history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raised immunoglobulin E, positive skin tests, dry patchy scales and infection susceptibility are the minor ones. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Through which receptor does amphetamine raise blood pressure?","choices":["Beta-two","Alpha-two","Dopaminergic","Alpha-one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with beta-one accounting for the cardiac stimulation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin provides methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage?","choices":["Ceftriaxone, a third generation agent","Ceftaroline, a fifth generation agent","Cefepime, a fourth generation agent","Ceftolozane/tazobactam, a fifth generation agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the cephalosporin that breaks the class's usual absence of methicillin-resistant coverage, and it must be renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which three systems make up the autonomic division?","choices":["Afferent, efferent and enteric","Central, peripheral and enteric","Enteric, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Somatic, parasympathetic and sympathetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The enteric system is the one most often forgotten of the three. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes ceftriaxone from most other cephalosporins in renal impairment?","choices":["It must be dialysed off before each subsequent dose","It requires no dosage adjustment in renal insufficiency","It requires the largest dose reduction of any cephalosporin in renal insufficiency","It is contraindicated at any degree of renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a useful contrast because the class as a whole is mostly renally eliminated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the collateral consequence of fluoroquinolone overuse?","choices":["Permanent loss of Gram-positive coverage across the entire class","Cross-resistance to all beta-lactams","High rates of resistance, and Clostridium difficile as collateral damage","Accelerated hepatic metabolism of co-administered drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Overuse equals resistance, and it is emphasised deliberately. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What proportion of patients must stop intravenous ganciclovir because of side effects?","choices":["About 1 in 20","Almost none; it is generally well tolerated","Essentially all patients","About a third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a striking figure, and the reason the better-absorbed oral prodrug matters clinically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which organisms cause the superficial mycoses of hair, nails and mucous membranes?","choices":["Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis","Candida, Coccidioides and Blastomyces","Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Aspergillus, Cryptococcus and Histoplasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These are the dermatophytes that griseofulvin and the allylamines treat. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which vascular effect does propranolol produce, and through which receptor?","choices":["Renal vasodilation, through dopaminergic receptors","Peripheral vasoconstriction, through beta-two blockade","Peripheral vasodilation, through alpha-one blockade","No vascular effect at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since blocking beta-two removes vasodilation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Why is an azole sometimes combined with a corticosteroid?","choices":["To prevent the development of resistance","To allow a shorter total course of therapy","To make the preparation less irritating to the skin","For more rapid symptom relief"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The steroid addresses symptoms while the antifungal addresses the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What should patients be advised about washing in acne?","choices":["Wash with a high pH soap several times daily, since a raised pH increases the activity of other topical agents","Avoid washing the affected area entirely while on treatment","Do not wash too frequently; twice a day, balancing cleanliness against drying and irritation","Wash as often as possible to remove surface oils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it adds that once rinsed off no active product remains on the skin. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which sequence describes neurotransmission at a cholinergic neuron?","choices":["Synthesis, storage, release, and inactivation by catechol-O-methyltransferase","Storage, release, binding, and permanent removal of choline from the neuron","Synthesis, storage, release, receptor binding, degradation, recycling of choline","Synthesis, release, reuptake into the presynaptic terminal, and repackaging without degradation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Six sequential steps, and several drug classes act at one of them. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why does voriconazole's intravenous formulation require caution in renal failure?","choices":["Its vehicle, cyclodextrin, accumulates when renal function is impaired","The drug itself is cleared entirely by glomerular filtration and reaches toxic levels","It crystallises in the renal tubule as acyclovir does","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the concern is the vehicle rather than the drug itself, which is an unusual reason to avoid a formulation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which patient should avoid topical tacrolimus or pimecrolimus?","choices":["A patient using an emollient","A patient with a weakened immune system","A patient with a history of acne","A patient over the age of sixty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and pairs this with a possible cancer risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How is norepinephrine given?","choices":["As a single intramuscular injection","Orally, in divided doses","By inhalation","As a titrated intravenous infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which suits a drug used to support vascular resistance in shock. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What kind of compound is oseltamivir as administered?","choices":["A prodrug converted to its carboxylate form","An active guanine analogue requiring no conversion","A monoclonal antibody against viral haemagglutinin","A live attenuated preparation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — another prodrug in a lecture full of them, alongside valacyclovir, famciclovir and valganciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A patient develops oral candidiasis after a course of broad-spectrum antibiotics. Which predisposing factor does this illustrate?","choices":["Suppression of competing organisms","Loss of barriers","Metabolic abnormality","A warm and moist local environment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Removing the bacterial flora leaves the niche open, which is why antibiotic exposure is itself a fungal risk factor. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What is the position on isotretinoin and pregnancy?","choices":["It is contraindicated only in the first trimester","It requires no pregnancy precautions because it is applied topically","It is contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding, and men should avoid it as well","It is safe in the second and third trimesters, and only the first trimester requires contraceptive cover"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the iPledge programme exists around exactly this risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for ganciclovir?","choices":["Blurred vision and mental confusion from enzyme induction","Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Seizures, delirium and tremor only","Peripheral neuropathy and tendon injury"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, occurring in a minority of patients alongside the haematologic effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"How well does ciclopirox nail lacquer work for onychomycosis?","choices":["It works only on fingernails, not toenails","It is as effective as oral terbinafine","It is less than twelve per cent effective","It is curative in the majority of patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and saying so up front is what keeps expectations honest for a months-long treatment. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which of the two prevents cardiovascular mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Carvedilol","Labetalol","Propranolol","Pindolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases lipid peroxidation and vascular wall thickening. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How do the indirect-acting cholinergic agents work?","choices":["They block reuptake of acetylcholine into the presynaptic terminal","They increase the activity of acetylcholine by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","They bind muscarinic receptors directly and mimic acetylcholine","They increase synthesis of acetylcholine from acetyl coenzyme A and choline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They raise the transmitter rather than binding the receptor themselves. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which of the listed antiviral approaches works on the host rather than on the virus?","choices":["Inhibiting viral protein synthesis","Stimulating the host immune system","Inhibiting specific viral enzymes","Blocking viral assembly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the only one of the eight aimed at the host side of the interaction. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What does nicotine do at the nicotinic receptor as concentration rises?","choices":["It has no effect on the nicotinic receptor, acting only at muscarinic sites","It stimulates the receptor at low concentrations and blocks it at high concentrations","It stimulates the receptor at all concentrations, increasing with dose","It blocks the receptor at all concentrations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why nicotine appears in both the agonist and the ganglionic blocker discussions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is metronidazole's mechanism of action?","choices":["It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase","It interacts with bacterial DNA to cause loss of helical structure and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","It binds the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks the transpeptidation step of protein synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its activity spans Gram-positive and Gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What does the afferent division do, and what is an example?","choices":["It connects preganglionic to postganglionic neurons within a ganglion","It controls gastrointestinal motility independently of the brain","It carries signals to the central nervous system","It carries signals away from the central nervous system to skeletal muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reflex arc needs the incoming limb before any autonomic outflow can be adjusted. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does reserpine act?","choices":["It blocks transmitter uptake into vesicles","It blocks release of stored norepinephrine from vesicles","It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It inhibits monoamine oxidase within the neuron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, depleting norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What is intravenous labetalol used for?","choices":["Hypertensive emergencies","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute asthma","Septic shock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, using its combined alpha and beta blockade. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Why is polymyxin resistance uncommon, and why is use expected to increase?","choices":["The class went largely unused for the last 50 years, and rising multidrug resistance is bringing it back despite optimal regimens not being well studied","The mechanism is impossible for bacteria to circumvent, and newer formulations have removed the nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity entirely","It is used only topically, so systemic resistance cannot emerge","It is always combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor that prevents resistance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low historical exposure is the reason resistance never developed widely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Besides blocking norepinephrine reuptake, what else does cocaine do?","choices":["It inhibits monoamine oxidase","It depletes vesicular stores of norepinephrine","It stimulates dopaminergic receptors in the kidney","It blocks neuronal sodium channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also blocks sodium-potassium activated ATPase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which agents are the gold standard for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Sedating antihistamines","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the agent chosen by severity and by site of disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which azoles are noted as teratogenic?","choices":["None of the azoles carries a teratogenicity caution","Only the topical imidazole derivatives","Itraconazole, and fluconazole and voriconazole in animals","Ketoconazole and posaconazole only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reproductive safety is a recurring caution across the azole group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What are atropine's effects on the eye?","choices":["No ocular effect at any dose","Persistent mydriasis and cycloplegia","Miosis with spasm of accommodation","Reduced aqueous humour production with no change in pupil size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the opposite of the miosis and accommodation spasm the agonists cause. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which features of fungal cells explain why antibacterial antibiotics do not treat them?","choices":["They replicate only inside host cells, so extracellular antibiotics never reach them","They are eukaryotic, with a rigid cell wall containing chitin, a cell membrane built on ergosterol, different ribosomes and a distinct nuclear membrane","They are prokaryotic, with a peptidoglycan cell wall and a 70S ribosome that antibacterial agents cannot reach, and a nuclear region without a surrounding membrane","They lack a cell wall entirely and rely on a cholesterol membrane, so cell wall agents have no target"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every antifungal class exploits one of these differences, which is also why selectivity is harder than with bacteria. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What happens to newly formed influenza virions when neuraminidase is inhibited?","choices":["Their nucleic acid cannot be replicated","They cannot be cleaved free of the host cell as budding progeny","They can no longer attach to the receptor on the next host cell","They cannot uncoat once inside the next cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they remain tethered at the surface, so the infection cannot spread to new cells. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which additional effect does carbachol produce through its nicotinic action?","choices":["Release of epinephrine from the adrenal medulla","Direct blockade of the neuromuscular junction","Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase","Depletion of stored norepinephrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why its cardiovascular and gastrointestinal effects can be profound and biphasic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient is agitated and hallucinating, with dilated pupils, flushed dry skin, fever, urinary retention and a fast heart. Which toxidrome is this?","choices":["Nicotinic, from ganglionic stimulation","Beta blocker overdose","Anticholinergic","Muscarinic, from a cholinergic agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Remember it as mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, dry as a bone, hot as Hades, with retention and tachycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where are adrenergic receptors located relative to the synapse?","choices":["Only postsynaptically on the effector organ","Only presynaptically on the neuron","Only within the central nervous system","Presynaptically or postsynaptically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what alpha-two agonists exploit. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which drug interaction of sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim involves cytochrome P450 2C9 inhibition?","choices":["Reduced effectiveness of oral contraceptives through gut flora disruption","Serotonin syndrome with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors","Chelation reducing absorption of iron supplements","A significant increase in the international normalised ratio in patients on warfarin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Other interactions include raised phenytoin, rifampin and digoxin levels, reduced methotrexate clearance, and hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which toxicities define the aminoglycoside class?","choices":["Renal toxicity and ototoxicity, requiring renal dose adjustment","Hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Serotonin syndrome, hypertensive crisis, and thrombocytopenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and vancomycin shares both, which is why the combination is watched carefully. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Aminoglycosides are the drug of choice for which situations?","choices":["Clostridium difficile colitis","Febrile neutropenia, sepsis, and enterococcal synergy","Community-acquired pneumonia and acute otitis media","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with Enterococcus covered only at synergy dosing rather than as monotherapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why are fever and chills expected during an amphotericin B infusion, and how are they managed?","choices":["They are caused by rapid fungal lysis releasing endotoxin into the circulation, and are prevented by slowing the rate of the infusion alone","They are a disulfiram-like reaction to the infusion vehicle, and are avoided by withholding alcohol","They are driven by interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor, and patients are pretreated with acetaminophen, antihistamines and corticosteroids","They reflect an immunoglobulin E mediated allergic reaction, and the drug must be stopped permanently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reaction is a cytokine response rather than an allergy, which is why premedication works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is the approximate rate of penicillin cross-sensitivity with cephalosporins?","choices":["Approximately 10 percent","Approximately 25 percent","Cross-sensitivity does not occur at all between the two classes","Less than 1 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The figure matters clinically because a reported penicillin allergy does not automatically exclude a cephalosporin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is mupirocin used for beyond ordinary skin infection?","choices":["Treating onychomycosis of the toenails","Eliminating nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Eliminating vulvovaginal candidiasis in women","Treating recurrent orolabial herpes simplex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its coverage is most gram positive aerobes, especially meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What happens to cardioselectivity at higher doses?","choices":["It increases","It is unchanged","It converts to alpha blockade","It is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so a beta-one selective agent can still affect the airway. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"A corticosteroid is not controlling a plaque. What does the concentration gradient predict?","choices":["Raising the concentration increases drug transfer and can overcome the resistance","Concentration has little effect once the stratum corneum is saturated","Changing to a more drying vehicle will matter more than concentration","Only a change to a systemic agent will overcome it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroid resistance overcome by increased concentration is the worked example. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why give a small dose of a nondepolarizing blocker before succinylcholine?","choices":["To prolong the duration of the block","To prevent malignant hyperthermia","To allow a lower dose of anaesthetic agent","To prevent the fasciculations that cause muscle soreness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a comfort measure that addresses a predictable adverse effect. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which penicillin dosage form is given intramuscularly as a single long-acting dose?","choices":["Penicillin V potassium (Pen VK)","Penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)","Ampicillin sodium","Penicillin G benzathine (Bicillin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The benzathine salt is the depot form, which is why one injection can cover an indication such as syphilis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which adverse reactions are listed for the echinocandins?","choices":["Visual disturbance and hepatic dysfunction","Bone marrow suppression and hepatotoxicity as the principal dose-limiting effects","Renal tubule damage requiring saline hydration","Tachycardia, headache, insomnia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and blood dyscrasias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note the electrolyte disturbances overlap with amphotericin B's, despite a completely different mechanism. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which three classes make up the cholinergic antagonists?","choices":["Antimuscarinic agents, ganglionic blockers and neuromuscular blocking agents","Direct-acting agents, indirect-acting agents and reactivators","Alpha blockers, beta blockers and mixed blockers","Choline esters, alkaloids and anticholinesterases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are distinguished by which cholinergic site they occupy. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which of ganciclovir and valganciclovir is the better-absorbed oral form?","choices":["Both are equally absorbed","Neither is absorbed orally; both are intravenous only","Valganciclovir, the prodrug","Ganciclovir, the parent compound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ganciclovir's oral bioavailability is very low, which is why the prodrug exists. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Voriconazole is derived from which other azole?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Clotrimazole","Fluconazole","Itraconazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a fluconazole derivative introduced in 2002, which then took over systemic aspergillosis from amphotericin B. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which receptors does oxymetazoline act on?","choices":["Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one and beta-two","Alpha-one only","Dopaminergic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, producing vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which are the three polyene agents?","choices":["Ketoconazole, miconazole and clotrimazole","Griseofulvin, terbinafine and naftifine","Amphotericin B, nystatin and natamycin","Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amphotericin B is the systemic agent; nystatin is used topically and orally because it is poorly absorbed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which infusion-related reaction is characteristic of vancomycin?","choices":["Tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture","Red man syndrome, along with fever, chills and phlebitis","Disulfiram-like reaction when combined with ethanol","Serotonin syndrome when combined with a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity among the monitoring concerns. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does oseltamivir represent?","choices":["Inhibiting viral release","Blocking viral attachment to cells","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Stimulating the host immune system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — neuraminidase frees budding progeny, so blocking it traps new virus at the cell surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"With which agents is flucytosine combined, and for what?","choices":["With amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis, and with itraconazole in chromoblastomycosis","With caspofungin in oesophageal candidiasis, and with terbinafine in onychomycosis","With griseofulvin in tinea capitis, and with nystatin in oral candidiasis","With voriconazole in invasive aspergillosis, and with fluconazole in vaginal candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is used in combination rather than alone, and its own indications are Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which conditions make up the atopic triad?","choices":["Asthma and allergic rhino-conjunctivitis","Psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis","Urticaria and angio-oedema","Acne vulgaris and rosacea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and skin barrier dysfunction is what says plays the major role in the skin component. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 33"}]};
